WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
React Native does not reinvent how mobile hardware functions.

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.140
When you write JavaScript for requesting location data or reading a sensor,

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:14.340
you are utilizing the same Native infrastructure that has governed iOS for years.

4
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.620
Access is controlled through established frameworks like Core Location for GPS,

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.900
Core Motion for telemetry, and Core Bluetooth for external device connectivity.

6
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.700
The relationship is visible in the syntax.

7
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.060
On the left is the native Swift implementation using CL Location Manager Delegate.

8
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.980
On the right is the React Native version using Expo Location.

9
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.860
If we remove the language specific syntax, the structural skeletons match.

10
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.780
The native delegate pattern and completion handlers are translated directly into JavaScript promises.

11
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.660
React Native libraries act as thin wrappers,

12
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.780
routing data from the system to your app components.

13
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.660
This means native delegate models and update patterns translate into standard JavaScript

14
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.140
async functions and event subscriptions.

15
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.020
Notting these connections reveals how the native iOS mental model applies directly

16
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.940
to the JavaScript layer, making hardware integration more predictable.

17
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.300
This integration begins with permission-gated APIs.

18
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.420
Core Location serves as the system-level gateway for GPS tracking and geofencing.

19
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.380
A request originates at the hardware, passes through the native CL Location Manager,

20
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.700
and is surfaced by Expo Location.

21
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.940
But specific OS-level keys are mandatory in info.plist.

22
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.180
For foreground location, NS location when-in-use usage description

23
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.780
must be declared to allow hardware data to route to the application.

24
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.220
If this key is missing, the request fails at the system level.

25
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.140
The permission dialog will not appear,

26
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.700
and the API request will fail silently.

27
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.900
Background location requires a two-step configuration.

28
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.220
Apple mandates the NS location always and when-in-use usage description entry

29
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.140
to enable tracking while suspended.

30
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.620
Core Bluetooth follows a similar requirement.

31
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:03.460
The React Native BLE-PLX package relies on the NS Bluetooth always usage description key

32
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.500
for scanning and connecting on iOS 13 and later.

33
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.620
Bluetooth also requires explicit resource management.

34
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.060
When a component unmounts,

35
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.940
the central manager must be destroyed to release the underlying CB Central Manager resources.

36
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.020
Regardless of the JavaScript syntax,

37
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.580
hardware access is governed by these native configuration files and lifecycle constraints.

38
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.900
Moving beyond simple discrete requests,

39
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.180
sensors like the accelerometer provide continuous telemetry,

40
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.140
a constant flowing stream of high-frequency data.

41
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.540
On iOS, this is handled by Core Motion,

42
00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.540
with CM Motion Manager as the entry point for data from the accelerometer,

43
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.820
the gyroscope, the barometer, and the pedometer.

44
00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.380
Expo sensors wraps CM Motion Manager,

45
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.500
separating each sensor into independent JS modules.

46
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.660
These sensors require an active data pipeline flowing from hardware to application.

47
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:56.580
The native start accelerometer updates method maps directly to JavaScript's ad listener,

48
00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.820
transforming a continuous native stream into a standard event subscription.

49
00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.260
Most sensors stream without specific keys,

50
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.500
but the pedometer is an exception.

51
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:11.860
CM Pedometer requires the NS Motion Usage description key in the app configuration.

52
00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.340
These continuous native pipelines

53
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.260
match to standard event listeners,

54
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.340
allowing sensor data to be managed within the normal React lifecycle.

55
00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.020
A synchronous hardware evaluation handles critical binary states,

56
00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.700
such as instantly confirming a user's identity through biometric scans,

57
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.700
like Face ID or Touch ID.

58
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.260
The configuration rules apply here as well.

59
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.660
NS Face ID Usage description must be present in the info.p list

60
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.460
before the system permits an authentication prompt.

61
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:45.300
The Expo Local Authentication Library wraps the native LA context.evaluate policy method,

62
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.580
providing a simple async call to the JavaScript thread.

63
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:54.740
The library maps native LA error codes to JavaScript strings to handle failure states.

64
00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:00.500
When a user cancels the prompt, the native user cancel returns as user cancel.

65
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.380
If disabled by too many attempts, biometry lockout maps to lockout,

66
00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.540
triggering a UI fallback.

67
00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:11.460
This mapping ensures that hardware evaluation is transparent,

68
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.300
providing native error codes that allow for precise UI decisions.

69
00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.380
Finally, we see this mapping in UI-driven hardware,

70
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.900
such as map components and physical feedback generators.

71
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.060
React native maps utilizes UKIT's MKMAP view.

72
00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.020
By using the provider default setting on iOS,

73
00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.820
the library binds directly to Apple Maps.

74
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.500
Native map configurations are exposed through string properties.

75
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:42.180
Passing a value to the map type prop updates the underlying MKMAP type on the native view.

76
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:48.100
For example, the native .satellite flyover enum is accessed by passing the string satellite

77
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:55.780
flyover. haptic feedback follows the same pattern. Expo haptics wraps the three UI-KIT generators,

78
00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:01.060
impact, notification, and selection into named JavaScript functions.

79
00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.740
This design handles cross-platform differences automatically.

80
00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.500
If executed on an Android device lacking the equivalent engine,

81
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.460
the library safely treats them as a no-op.

82
00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.220
This ensures the same code remains functional across platforms,

83
00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.220
as haptic calls are simply ignored on devices that lack the hardware support.

84
00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.820
This architectural relationship between the JavaScript thread and the underlying native system

85
00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.540
solidifies into a highly predictable, reliable roadmap for developers.

86
00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:35.540
Integrating hardware in React Native is effectively an exercise in native iOS development,

87
00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.580
performed through a streamlined abstraction layer.

88
00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.260
This table maps iOS frameworks to react native counterparts.

89
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:48.820
Native frameworks like CL Location Delegate, CB Central Manager, and LA Context

90
00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:56.100
perform the actual device logic. React native libraries like Expo Location and React Native BLE

91
00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:03.380
plugs serve as direct links to those native engines. Success depends on respecting native constraints,

92
00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:08.260
managing the lifecycle of managers, and ensuring all info.plist permissions

93
00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:15.060
are correctly defined. By treating the native OS as the engine and JavaScript as the interface,

94
00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:20.340
you can build hardware integrations that are as stable and performant as their native counterparts.

